    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 152145\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n245 PM PDT Thu Oct 15 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Gusty north to northeast winds will continue this afternoon into \n  Friday morning, with gusts of 40 to 50mph across portions of the \n  region.\n\n* Precipitation chances return to the region Tuesday and Wednesday.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Through next Thursday.\n\nGusty north to northeast winds will continue across the region this \nafternoon into Friday as a shortwave continues to dig south along \nthe backside of a broad upper-level trough over the Rocky Mountains. \nThe previous forecast remains on track, with gusts of 25 to 35mph \nbeing observed across much of the region. Given the northeast \ndirection of the strong winds, enhanced downslope wind gusts as high \nas 50mph are possible west of some of the higher terrain, including \nthe Pahrump Valley and areas west of the Hualapai Mountains in \nArizona. Strong crosswinds could make driving difficult on I-40 west \nof the Hualapai Mountains and Highway 160 near Pahrump. No changes \nhave been made to the Wind Advisory, which remains in effect through \n11 AM PDT Friday. Winds will diminish Friday afternoon as the trough \npulls away. The most significant wind impacts should ease by midday, \nalthough breezy northeast to east winds gusting 25 to 35mph are \nexpected through the afternoon. This downward trend will continue \nSaturday, with light winds returning Sunday onward. After a brief \nwarmup to near normal this afternoon and Friday, temperatures will \ndrop a few degrees to slightly below normal for the weekend.\n\nDry conditions will persist through at least the weekend before \nattention turns to how a deep West Coast trough interacts with \nremnant moisture potentially moving into the region from Hurricane \nSimon. Currently, moisture levels do not look to be as high as what \nwe saw with the remnants of Rachel earlier this week, but the \nimpacts that will be seen in our region remain uncertain. The GFS \nand ECMWF have different solutions on Integrated Vapor Transport \n(IVT) plume locations and how they interact with the incoming \ntrough. The GFS has 300 to 500kg/m/s IVT values over the lower \nColorado River Valley Monday evening, while the ECMWF has values of \n250 to 400kg/m/s at the same time, with the higher values displaced \nfarther west over the western Mojave Desert. No major changes to the \nprecipitation forecast have been made with this forecast issuance. \nChances remain 30 to 40 percent Tuesday into Wednesday across much \nof the area, with 20 percent chances lingering into Thursday. Snow \nlevels will be 8000ft to 9000ft, so some light snow accumulations in \nthe higher elevations of southern Nevada are possible, although \nconfidence in these details remains low given how far out this \nsystem is in the forecast. With the incoming system and the \npotential for rain, temperatures will remain near or below normal in \nthe extended period. \n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 00Z Forecast Package...Clear \nskies with breezy to strong winds last through the TAF period. \nNortheast winds are becoming more northerly and will reduce slightly \nfrom the stronger gusts observed this afternoon, peaking around 24 \nkts. These winds will continue into early Friday before reducing \nagain, but remaining breezy. Strong northeast winds return late \nFriday morning with gusts approaching 30 kts. Gusts and winds reduce \nagain that afternoon before winds ease and gusts cut off into Friday \nevening.\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 00Z Forecast Package...Clear skies last through \nthe TAF period. Due to continued sheltering and its position in \nrelation to the upper level pattern, KBIH keeps mostly light to \nmoderate winds, while the rest of the region see strong North to \nNortheast winds. Gusty north and Northeast winds have arrived across \nthe region, with gusts above 30 kts possible at most TAF sites into \nthe evening (excluding KBIH of course. Winds reduce into the evening \nand early night but remain breezy to gusty for all but KDAG and \nKBIH. northerly winds increase again for KEED and KIFP in the late \nnight, while the rest of the region (with exception of KBIH, which \nstays light) holds off until the mid to late morning before strong \nwinds build back into the area. Winds begin to ease in the mid \nafternoon for KEED and KIFP, with other sites seeing winds drop off \nslightly beyond the TAF period.\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Planz\nAVIATION...Murdock\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
